# “Divelara” in a Desperate Scramble
Tamil title: டிவேலரா திணறல்

## Danger! Warning!

Congress itself descends from its position and calls upon Britain. Ignoring even Gandhi's doctrine and leadership, Congress yearns to assist Britain in the war.

Congress newspapers have begun crying their auctioneer's call: “If this moment is missed, another opportunity will be difficult to obtain. Time once gone will not return or stand still.” Achariyar's millions of disciples perform penance so that his desire may be fulfilled. But can it happen? Will the British appoint Congressmen as ministers merely because they have abandoned nonviolence? If Achariyar's group declares that it has escaped Gandhi's restraint and guard, will the British step forward and embrace it? They will not. That is why we wrote yesterday that Achariyar was going to discover defeat within victory.

Congressmen may shout “Danger! Warning!” But anyone who looks closely can see clearly who is in danger and who requires warning. The British have said that they can speak or act only if Gandhi agrees. Will they now value or fear a Congress that has removed Gandhi and passed a resolution opposed by Babu Rajendra Prasad, Acharya Kripalani and others? Those who did not fear when both mortar and pestle arrived have no reason to fear the rest.

The All India Congress Committee has 365 members. Only 182 attended the Poona meeting. Why did the others not come?

When the French Parliament assembled under the point of Hitler's spear to crown Pétain, many members stayed away because they did not wish to participate in the act of surrender. Likewise, many did not attend at Poona.

Did all, or even an overwhelming majority, of those who attended support Achariyar's resolution? Let the figures speak. After eight hours of debate, 95 supported it, 47 opposed it and 40 remained neutral. Fear, revulsion, indifference or bewilderment must have led those forty to neutrality; plainly they did not support Achariyar. In reality his victory rested on only eight excess votes: eight hours of argument and eight votes beyond the opposing and neutral total. Such was the ornament of Achariyar's victory.

Will the British yield to this? Let Achariyar answer. Anyone with intelligence would say that no one will value a victory won through such struggling and gasping, obtained after betraying Gandhi, the leader.

The *Indian Express* wrote that no one need thank Achariyar for this service and that his conduct deserved no gratitude. Seeing the tenor of his Poona speech, militants indignantly asked why he surrendered so openly merely for office.

## He Dug Through a Mountain and Caught a Mouse

Thus Achariyar succeeded in passing his resolution against Gandhi, though many Working Committee members disliked it, many opposed it, Gandhians grew angry and militants mocked him. He dug through a mountain and caught a mouse.

Is Achariyar ashamed? No. In the Tamil film *Bhakta Thyagaraja*, a Brahmin enters a banana grove and steals fruit. The gardener catches him, strings the stolen plantains into a garland and parades him upon a donkey. The Brahmin calls his wife, hands her the garland and says: “Woman, make this into *podimas*. Only two streets remain; I shall finish the procession and return.” He cares nothing for shame; obtaining the plantains is enough.

People ask whether the deed matters more or valour. Aryanism considers only the deed and disregards honour and indignation. The learned man riding the donkey illustrates that doctrine. Though Achariyar's Poona victory is something of which he himself ought to be ashamed, as a son of Aryanism he will not care. Will the *podimas* not taste good? Whether the fruit was stolen or purchased, food eaten free of charge remains tasty; will it become bitter merely because it arrived through a donkey procession?

Likewise, what matters to him if he won by eight votes, Frontier Gandhi despised it, comrades rejected it or Gandhi grew angry? The resolution passed, and that satisfies him. If it also brings office, he will plunge into an ocean of bliss. “Do not care about the method; attend only to the outcome” is the instruction given by the Aryan ancestor Manu, and Achariyar holds the same view.

His personal state, however, is not Britain's only concern. Even if the British wished to give Congress what it asked, the obstacle was not merely the doctrine of nonviolence.

## Making Achariyar Weighty Is No Light Matter

Congressmen still cannot openly tell people in every part of the country, “Give neither money nor men.” Covert propaganda is being conducted, but it has no effect.

Politics is peaceful now; there is no party conflict. During Congress's twenty-seven months in office, the country heard the noise of a house in mourning and experienced opposition and repression. They are absent today. If Congress returns to office, will this peace remain? Could the Muslim League, the Dravidian movement and other groups live peacefully under Congress rule? Could Congress govern in a manner that allowed them to do so?

People now know Congress's disposition well. It turned a deaf ear, compelled the singing of *Vande Mataram*, caused Thalamuthu's wife to lose her marriage pendant, and its ruler Achariyar threatened to strike with whatever lay at hand. Others treated submission to him as political strategy. If these people return during wartime, will they govern so that peace prevails here?

Britain concluded that Neville Chamberlain's rule produced divisions in Parliament and quarrels in the country. It made him resign, installed Churchill and sought government without internal discord. Would the British now purchase trouble by restoring a Congress that knew only how to rule in a manner that destroyed the country's peace?

We say this because we trust that the British retain some political intelligence. However many somersaults Congress leaders perform, and however much they reduce their demand day by day, the British will not restore them to office before Congress obtains the help, cooperation, permission, blessing and support of the other people of this country. After witnessing Congress quarrel internally at Poona, Britain will be still less willing to place Congressmen in government.

The Poona split reveals how much anger against Britain exists among many Congressmen. Congress newspapers therefore warn that, if Britain does not meet Achariyar's group's request, those who opposed him at Poona will gain strength and Britain's situation will grow difficult. That is the basis of their cries of danger and warning.

## Will Britain Fear a Threat from Spindle Troops?

No one will care about this bogey. Whether one faction within Congress falls and another rises, or the whole Congress roars in one breath, Britain will not be frightened.

The age when threats produced fear is past. Can Congress intimidate Britain more than Hitler? Hitler declares that he has a secret weapon whose use would ruin the whole world and tells Britain to beware. Yet Britain remains unshaken.

Leave verbal threats aside. According to today's newspaper, Germany suddenly attacked Dover with thirty Junkers aircraft, eighty-seven dive bombers and fifty fighters, 167 aircraft in all. Bombs fell. Did the British panic? No. Within thirty minutes they brought down fifteen German aircraft and the rest fled.

Would a British government unafraid of Hitler's speeches and barbarous warfare fear the threats of troops armed with spindles? Are the newspapers warning Britain because a private Congress army is about to set out, spinning as it marches?

And what of money? Is money not already arriving without Congress's favour? Does the government not know that Congressmen themselves donated money while asking that it not be disclosed publicly? What, then, would make the government swoon before Congress?

If Congress threatens civil disobedience and agitation unless its demand is accepted, will the government collapse? No. It will point to Britain, where Sir Oswald Mosley, an aristocrat and relative of a former Viceroy, was placed behind bars.

Can Congress even conduct civil disobedience? Gandhi, its creator, has said it cannot be done and will not occur. Congress knows that the British government did not hesitate to use severe measures within Britain itself to suppress agitation. Britain will therefore not fear civil disobedience. For Congress, the horoscope of civil disobedience has always ended beneath the baleful influence of Saturn in the eighth house. That threat too will achieve nothing.

If Germany springs upon Britain, how many days of war expenditure could India supply? A single day's war now costs fifty-seven million pounds. If an aircraft is destroyed, a lakh of rupees burns; if a large ship sinks, a crore disappears. Amid expenditure of this magnitude, how many days could India bear even if it practised alchemy?

Congress journals may continue shouting “Danger! Warning!” Anyone who observes carefully will nevertheless understand who is in danger and who needs the warning.

## What Does the Poona Resolution Reveal?

It reveals that division within Congress, decline in Gandhism, betrayal within Congress and Congress's craving for office have become abundant.

At Poona, Achariyar demonstrated the deformity of the doctrine of nonviolence. Yet office-mania is implicit in every word of his demonstration.

The British have now understood the agitation of Achariyar's group: it roams so desperately for office that it dared split Congress, abandon Gandhism and even surrender Gandhi's leadership. Britain will refuse to respect people who pant after office.

Nor is Congress the country's sole representative even if it unanimously presents a demand with Gandhi included. Are there no other parties and no people with other ideals? Some opposed Achariyar's resolution because they feared Hitler's spear; the British may take comfort from that. But the British also know the path for maintaining peace in the country: even if Congress leaders perform every kind of somersault and diminish their demand daily, Congress cannot return to office without the aid, agreement and support of the country's other communities.

*Viduthalai*, 31 July 1940.

---NOTES---
The opening scan confirms `டிவேலரா திணறல்`; OCR had split and damaged it as `டி வேலரா .தஇணறல்`. The exact referent or derivation of `டிவேலரா` remains uncertain, so it is retained as “Divelara” rather than assigned an unsupported meaning. `திணறல்` indicates struggling, gasping or desperate floundering.

The supplied source is materially disordered. Image 002 is the ending of the preceding article `வெற்றியில் வீழ்ச்சி`; images 003 and 004 duplicate one another; image 008 repeats the opening of that passage before continuing it; and later sections overlap across images 005–010. The English excludes the clearly unrelated previous-article page and presents every distinct passage belonging to this article once in a coherent sequence. The bilingual builder nevertheless retains the entire corrected canonical Tamil OCR block verbatim.

The numerical claim `570 லட்சம்பவுன்` is translated according to its literal arithmetic as fifty-seven million pounds. Historical wartime figures and the article's claims about political actors are preserved as the author's statements and have not been independently validated.

`தக்ளி` is a hand spindle and is used mockingly in “spindle troops.” `பொடிமாஸ்` is a mashed or crumbled vegetable preparation. References to “Aryanism,” Manu, Congress and other movements retain the article's polemical historical vocabulary.
